Dexter the twisted tale of a serial  killer with a moral code was a massive  ratings hit for Showtime it was also an  influential series that prompted a slew  of similarly lethal anti-heroes on TV  now it's time to conduct an autopsy on  the show's conclusion when Dexter was  just two years old his mother was  brutally killed at the hands of a cartel  boss and he and his brother were left  sitting in her blood  at first the audience is encouraged to  believe that this foundational trauma is  what put Dexter down the path to being a  serial killer  when Dexter's adopted father Harry  realizes his son lacks emotional empathy  and might possibly be a sociopath like  when he finds Dexter killing  neighborhood pets  he teaches Dexter a code so the child  won't get caught the code of Harry my  foster father is satisfied but by the  end of Dexter his story is far more  complicated as it turns out it was a  criminal psychiatrist who had come up  with the code Harry was only responsible  for teaching it to Dexter and it wasn't  until Dexter's first kill that he  finally understood the monster he'd  helped create it would be years before  Dexter learned that his father's death  had been at his own hands once he  realized what his adopted son was  capable of one of the most compelling  things about Dexter is his incredible  level of self-awareness  according to professionals it's this  introspection that makes him an  unrealistic psychopath in spite of being  so relatable to viewers he struggles in  society just as we all do but in  Dexter's case he struggles with feeling  empathy and love even though he  understands what it looks like just like  me empty inside as the narrative  progresses from the beginning to the end  of Dexter we see him go from someone who  must imitate human emotion to a man who  actually does feel and comes to truly  care about those closest to him in fact  it's this emotional development that  causes Dexter to get so many people  around him hurt or killed in the  anthology the psychology of Dexter  author Christopher Ryan writes Dexter is  both cold-blooded killer and  warm-hearted family man  unfeeling assassin and supportive friend  like all of us he is confused yet  he's the best and worst we can be often  simultaneously  Dexter's audience is so focused on what  he'll get away with next that a key  aspect of the show often gets overlooked  if America's criminal justice system  actually worked better and criminals  weren't set free by means of legal  loopholes or skilled defense attorneys  there would be no need for vigilantes  like Dexter so when he sets his sights  on unfairly freed predators Dexter is  highlighting a real life crisis in  America and in a way his actions are  making the world a little bit safer for  women and children on the flip side  Dexter also shows us how easy it is to  game the law enforcement system from the  inside Harry's code insists that the  person Dexter targets must be a killer  or other kind of confirmed monster but  since Dexter is able to change  information in a computer database it  only takes a few keystrokes to release  someone as his own prey on the occasion  he gets bored and needs a victim this  system is how Dexter eventually  identifies the men responsible for his  mother's death and because of his  easygoing persona there are few people  who ever strongly suspect something is  off with Dexter at least until much  later on in this story  Perry's code defines Dexter's approach  to vigilante justice but it isn't  foolproof and the more human Dexter  becomes through his relationships both  at home and at work  the more he exposes the innocent people  around him to the collateral damage of  his targets on top of that Dexter  happens to be a consummate risk taker  and his exploits get more and more  dangerous as the years go by in season  two there sergeant Doakes the first  person outside of Dexter's family to  suspect there's something wrong with him  Doakes is killed by Dexter's  ex-girlfriend Lila who does it in an  effort to protect him what's worse at  least for Doakes is that Dexter manages  to pin his own crimes on the detective  ruining his stellar record posthumously  nobody goes to Doakes funeral in season  3 Dexter unleashes a real monster with  district attorney Miguel Prado when  Prado finds out who Dexter really is he  wants to learn Harry's code the code  winds up meaning nothing however and  Prado goes on to kill a rival attorney  who is about to expose his corruption by  season 7  Dexter's lieutenant Maria LaGuerta  learns his true identity and launches an  investigation into Doakes innocence  ultimately it forces Dexter sister Deb  to choose between him and her colleague  Deb chooses her brother but not without  great personal loss but arguably the  worst death that Dexter has set in  motion is that of his eventual wife Rita  throughout season 4 Dexter engages in a  push and pull with Arthur Miller aka the  Trinity killer who targets victims based  on their similarities to his own family  although Dexter is ultimately successful  in putting Arthur on his table he leaves  room for Rita to be the last  Trinity kill and in a symbolic turn of  events that has since been named one of  the most shocking television deaths of  all time  Arthur leaves Dexter's son Harrison  sitting in a pool of his mother's blood  just as Dexter was when Harry found him  as a child other crime shows may wait  until later seasons before really amping  up the stakes but Dexter has his crimes  come to light by the show's second  season when divers find his dumping  ground off the Miami Coast dubbed the  Bay Harbor butcher  Dexter's true identity remains unknown  and because of his position in the  police department even assists in the  lab work for his own crimes this tension  does a great deal for Dexter's character  development right off the bat  Harry had taught him never to get caught  and because of his intellect  Dexter thought he had the system beat  but when his dumping ground is revealed  it's an enormous reality check that  grounds Dexter for a few years the Bay  Harbor butcher also marks a turning  point in Dexter's arc where collateral  deaths that don't meet the standard of  Harry's code begin in earnest Dexter  frames sergeant Doakes for his murders  although he has no intention of killing  him but Dexter's former lover Lila  becomes so obsessed with him that she's  driven to kill Doakes hopeful that  Dexter will love her in return she's  wrong and winds up being the next Bay  Harbor butcher victims like Hannibal  Lecter Dexter is one of the few serial  killers viewers actually root for even  after he gets innocent folks killed  Michael see Hall's fierce yet  compassionate performance is a big  reason  Dexter feels so human his internal  monologue courtesy of the Dexter writers  room is another but for some Dexter  isn't just someone to root for people  think they found their own personal  Batman people have actually committed  real-life crimes because they felt  related to him on such a deep level  maybe the show made Dexter a little bit  too real too sympathetic and too  relatable then again  Dexter's kill list is really really long  so now that we've seen the ending of  Dexter we should probably be thankful  more people haven't been inspired by his  laundry list of crimes at the beginning  of the show Dexter is convinced that he  can't be in a proper relationship  because physical intimacy would likely  reveal his inner monster to the other  person Rita seems perfect to him because  as an assault survivor she isn't  interested in the physical at all she's  perfect because Rita is in her own way  as damaged as me eventually however it  does become a factor in their  relationship and when Dexter joins  Narcotics Anonymous to provide a cover  for his activities he gets a beautiful  sponsor in Lila and discovers that he  actually is a physical person but only  with people who really see him things  are further complicated when Rita gets  pregnant and Dexter feels obligated to  play father both to that child and to  Rita's other kids  after Rita's tragic death Dexter is sure  he'll be alone for the rest of his life  and he sort of okay with it but then he  meets lumen the barrel girl survivor and  realizes that he misses having a partner  especially a partner who understands him  as deeply as Lila did Dexter ultimately  meets his match in Hannah McKay another  serial killer who falls in love with him  as he does with her for Dexter his  experience of both love and physical  intimacy comes from the women who  recognized and appreciate his  monstrousness and his humanity arguably  the most important woman in Dexter's  life is his adopted sister Debra Debra  is Dexter's grounding force constantly  reminding him of the good she sees in  him  in many ways Deborah is Dexter's living  conscience but for Debra the  relationship is far more complicated in  season one she winds up in the middle of  a bloody back in  forth between Dexter and his biological  brother another serial killer known as  the ice-truck killer he kidnaps and  tortures Deborah in an attempt to bond  with Dexter and the experience leaves  her traumatized and emotionally  dependent on her brother Deborah's  relationship bad luck would continue  throughout the series when another of  her boyfriends becomes the target of a  serial killer named the Skinner the  experience triggers Deborah's earlier  trauma she ultimately finds comfort in a  much older man and colleague but  tragically he too leaves her life when  he's shot and killed  Deborah's shoot-from-the-hip style  brings her career highs and lows from  vice to homicide detective to lieutenant  after she discovers her beloved brother  is a serial killer she joins the private  sector as an investigator complicating  things further Deborah realizes that her  feelings for Dexter are not just family  loves she goes against everything she  believes in when she kills LaGuerta to  save her brother the experience puts her  on the eventual downward spiral that  leads to her untimely death Dexter  Hannah and his son Harrison are just  about to ride off into the Buenos IDUs  sunset to live happily ever after when  Dexter gets sloppy again he leaves  fellow serial killer Oliver Saxon alive  in order for Debra to arrest him but  Oliver frees himself and shoots Deborah  in the stomach severely wounding her  when Dexter realizes that he's the one  responsible for Deborah getting shot and  put on life support he understands that  he will never have a happy ending he  sends Hannah and Harrison to Buenos IDs  without him takes Deborah off life  support and fakes his own death Dexter  had always worried Harrison would turn  out to be a monster like him in the end  Harrison is left to be raised by another  serial killer in another country with no  idea that his father is still alive  could this be the perfect setup for a  Dexter spinoff years down the road  featuring Harrison after the death of  his sister and the loss of his son and  lover  Dexter's internal monologue disappears  by the end of Dexter's eighth and final  season he is empty he has come to terms  of the fact that it's not safe for him  to be around people especially not  innocent people he loves lumberjack  Dexter lives alone in a  in the woods which begs the question as  he stopped killing he no longer has  access to a police database and can't  vet victims / Harry's code but  throughout the show Dexter makes it  clear that he doesn't have a choice in  killing he needs it to survive so after  the end of Dexter who and how is he  killing now the ambiguous closing shot  of a bearded Dexter silently staring  into the camera certainly leaves a lot  of room for future seasons check out one  of our newest videos right here plus  even more looper videos about your  favorite shows are coming soon subscribe  to our YouTube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

